    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> had the two temples cut into the mountainside in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, a source of gold and other trade goods, to impress upon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s might and to Egyptianise them.</li><li>Sand covered the Great Temple until European travellers reached it in the early 19th century; Belzoni's party entered it on 1 August 1817, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Champollion</a>'s reading of cartouches from drawings made there came in 1822.</li><li>In 1964 to 1968 the temples were cut into 1,036 blocks and rebuilt 65 metres higher and 208 metres inland, an operation that helped lead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>To save the Great Temple's colossi from the 30-ton lifting limit, the crowns were cut off, and each face was then cut away behind the ears; the first, a 19-ton face, was lifted by crane.</p></aside>
    </section>

