    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1264 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the temple complex began and lasted for about 20 years, until 1244 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1813</span><span class="rf-text">The Swiss researcher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> found the Small Temple and the top frieze of the Great Temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">Giovanni Belzoni's party entered the Great Temple on 1 August after 22 days of work</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1893</span><span class="rf-text">Royal Engineers removed about 850 long tons of dangerous rock from above the façade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1910</span><span class="rf-text">Alessandro Barsanti's clearing of the sand drift, begun in 1909, was completed, exposing a row of statues and the Chapel of Re-Harakhty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">An international campaign began to save the monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> from the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">The first block was lifted out of the Great Temple on 21 May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1968</span><span class="rf-text">The relocated complex was finished and formally inaugurated on 22 September</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii.jpg" alt="Ramesses II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_II_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pbuergler, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> embarked on an extensive building programme throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, which Egypt controlled. Nubia was a major source of gold and many other precious trade goods and was therefore of great importance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. He built several grand temples there in order to impress upon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> Egypt's might and to Egyptianise the people of Nubia. The most prominent were the rock-cut temples near the modern village of Abu Simbel, at the Second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> Cataract, the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-nubia">Upper Nubia</a>.</p><p>There are two temples. The Great Temple was dedicated to Ramesses II himself, and the Small Temple to his chief wife, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a>. Construction of the complex started in c. 1264 BCE and lasted for about 20 years, until 1244 BCE. It was known as the Temple of Ramesses, Beloved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. The Great Temple was dedicated to the gods Amun, Ra-Horakhty and Ptah, Egypt's three state deities of the time, as well as to the deified Ramesses, and the Small Temple to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, personified by Nefertari. Ra-Horakhty, Amun Ra and Ptah were the main divinities of that period, and their cult centres were at Heliopolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and Memphis respectively.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nefertari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nefertari.jpg" alt="Nefertari" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Nefertari_(52785688763).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Doudoroff, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Temple has a single entrance flanked by four colossal statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, each about 20 metres high, seated on a throne and wearing the double crown of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>. Beside the king's legs stand smaller statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a>, his mother Mut-Tuy, his first two sons and his first six daughters. The façade behind them is 33 metres high and 38 metres wide. It carries a frieze of twenty-two baboons worshipping the rising sun and a stele recording the marriage of Ramesses to a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a>, which sealed the peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>.</p><p>The hypostyle hall is supported by eight Osirid pillars depicting the deified Ramesses linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. Its walls bear battle scenes, much of the sculpture being given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, in which the king fought the Hittites; the most famous relief shows him on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> shooting arrows at fleeing enemies. A second pillared hall and a vestibule lead to the sanctuary, where four seated figures are cut into the rear wall: Ra-Horakhty, the deified king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> Ra and Ptah.</p><p>The Small Temple stands about 100 metres to the northeast. Its façade has statues of the king and queen of slightly more than 10 metres, and it is one of very few instances in Egyptian art where a king and his consort are shown at equal size.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii.jpg" alt="Psamtik II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Psamtik_II_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OliBac, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The temples were cut into the rock over about twenty years, and their alignment is the best-known mark of the builders' planning. It is believed that the temple's axis was positioned by the ancient Egyptian architects so that on 22 October and 22 February the sun's rays would penetrate the sanctuary and illuminate the sculptures on the back wall, except for the statue of Ptah, a god connected with the realm of the dead, who always remained in the dark. The dates are allegedly the king's birthday and coronation day, though there is no direct evidence to support this. One calculation, based on the heliacal rising of the star Sirius and on inscriptions, holds that the date must have been 22 October.</p><p>The date of the event would have been different when the temple was built, because of the drift of the Tropic of Cancer over about three millennia, and the temple was later moved, so the present alignment may not be as precise as the original. The phenomenon was first reported by Amelia Edwards in 1874, who judged that the effect was calculated and that the excavation was directed at one angle to produce it.</p><p>A Greek graffito on the left leg of the southern colossus records the passage of the soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>, led by Potasimto, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> by Amasis, and names Greek and Carian soldiers who scratched their names on the monument.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abu Simbel&#8221; cites where it covers Construction:</p><ul><li>Zahi Hawass, The Mysteries of Abu Simbel: Ramesses II and the Temples of the Rising Sun (The American University in Cairo Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt.jpg" alt="Johann Ludwig Burckhardt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Ludwig_Burckhardt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With time the temples fell into disuse, and the Great Temple became mostly covered by a sand dune. The temple remained unknown to Europeans until March 1813, when the Swiss researcher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> found the Small Temple and the top frieze of the Great Temple. In 1816 Giovanni Belzoni agreed a wage of two piastres a day per man with the local sheikh and exposed the head and shoulders of two colossi; he returned in 1817, and his party entered the Great Temple on 1 August after 22 days' work. In 1819 William John Bankes led a party that documented the interior.</p><p>Drawings made there reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Jean-François Champollion</a> on 14 September 1822, and the first cartouche he could decipher, connected to Ramesses, led, on his account, to his realisation that hieroglyphs mixed symbolic and phonetic signs. He and Ippolito Rosellini copied all the reliefs in 1828. David Roberts sketched the site in 1838, and Maxime Du Camp took the first photographs in 1850.</p><p>Visitors also left marks: Isabella Romer cut her name into the throne of the southern colossus in 1845, and Amelia Edwards's party inscribed theirs in the Chapel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, which Andrew McCallum found on 16 February 1874. Thomas Cook began a steamer service passing Abu Simbel in 1874.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam.jpg" alt="Aswan High Dam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aswan_High_Dam-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">شرف الدين, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In December 1892 the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Public Works found that overhanging rock threatened the colossi. A detachment of Royal Engineers worked from February to April 1893 and removed nearly 850 long tons of dangerous material, without accidents, at less than half the original estimate of £1,000. Between 1909 and 1910, under Alessandro Barsanti, the sand drift was permanently cleared. A row of statues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and the sacred hawk and the Chapel of Re-Harakhty came to light, and the colossi were stabilised with cement and iron pins.</p><p>In 1959 an international campaign began to save the monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>, whose lake would raise the water level at Abu Simbel by up to 60 metres. The sandstone would lose its strength once submerged. Several schemes were proposed, and in 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> preferred an Italian plan to lift the temples whole, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> adopted in 1963 a plan from the Swedish firm VBB to cut them into blocks, estimated at US$36 million, half the cost of the others.</p><p>After building a cofferdam, workers removed about 300,000 tons of rock, injected the stone with epoxy resin and cut the temples into 1,036 blocks, the last removed on 16 April 1966. The Great Temple was rebuilt 208 metres further inland and 65 metres higher, the Small Temple 67 metres higher, each under a concrete dome beneath an artificial hill. The project was completed in 1968 at a cost of US$41.7 million, about half borne by Egypt and half by contributions from 48 countries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion.jpg" alt="Jean-François Champollion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Jean-François Champollion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-François_Champollion,_by_Léon_Cogniet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Léon Cogniet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The relocation was considered at the time one of the greatest challenges of archaeological engineering in history. Its success helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> introduce a convention establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List, and the relocated complex belongs, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, Amada and Wadi es-Sebua, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> known as the Nubian Monuments. The team confirmed on 22 February 1966 that the sun's rays reached the statues of Ramesses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and Ra-Horakhty in the sanctuary while that of Ptah stayed in shadow, and the temples were oriented to repeat the two annual occasions, within a permitted deviation of 5 millimetres.</p><p>The site also shaped the study of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The drawings made there in 1819 served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Champollion</a> in deciphering hieroglyphs, and the photographs of Du Camp, Teynard and Frith in the 19th century belong to the early photographic record of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Early descriptions and drawings are found in Edward William Lane's Description of Egypt.</p><p>The temples remain a place where people gather on 22 October and 22 February to see the sun reach the sanctuary. They stand in the village of Abu Simbel on the western bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, about 230 km southwest of Aswan, in a hot desert climate.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Abu Simbel is a pair of rock-cut temples built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a> from about 1264 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Sand buried the Great Temple until European travellers reached it in the 19th century, and in 1964 to 1968 the temples were cut into blocks and rebuilt on higher ground above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>.</em></p>

